00:07:35.122 --> 00:07:35.830
LUKE STONE: Yeah.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:36.329
Got it.

00:07:36.329 --> 00:07:41.620
That is one of our main
things that we focused

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.810
on when we worked
with Home Depot

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.730
as Customer
Reliability Engineers,

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.770
is we tried to say,
OK, how can you

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:49.690
get your microservices
to run hot/hot

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:52.870
across multiple
regions and zones?

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:54.850
And this is something
that we do at Google

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:59.290
all the time because we do run
globally available systems.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:01.900
So we have some kind
of best practices

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.790
and good procedures for this.

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:06.100
And that's why we
have Mahesh here

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:07.870
to tell us a little
bit about how

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:09.970
that works for Google services.

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:13.840
He's an expert in our global
network and load balancing.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.850
MAHESH KALLAHALLA: Thanks, Luke.

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:19.120
So one of the things
when we designed

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.280
GCP, one of the things
we wanted to ensure

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.890
is try to make
Google [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.750
What does this mean?

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:29.310
So for instance, we
wanted to put in--

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:30.970
the infrastructure
that we built first,

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:34.429
we wanted to make sure
that it was easy to manage.

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:37.000
So it had all the APIs
to make managing it easy.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.309
We wanted to make sure that
it actually managed itself

00:08:39.309 --> 00:08:40.580
a little bit as well.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:43.510
And we also wanted
to ensure that it

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.682
incorporated a lot of
the best practices that

00:08:45.682 --> 00:08:47.640
were built running Google,
basically the Google

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:51.680
website, and Ads, and Gmail
over the last 15 years.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.250
That was actually bread and
butter when we were actually

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:56.530
thinking about designing GCP.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:09:02.230
So what I do here is
talk about an example

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:07.300
of how we incorporated some of
this specifically in the load

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:09.970
balancing product, and how
load balancing actually

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:14.280
plays a part in actually
providing reliability

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.690
beyond just pure load balancing.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:19.110
So a quick recap of how
load balancing works.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:23.050
At this basic front,
the GCLB product is--

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:27.570
it takes incoming requests,
and then forwards the request

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.520
to what looks like a proxy to
a whole bunch of, essentially,

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.750
instance groups are [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:34.990
The interesting
feature about GCLB,

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:37.780
and actually, this will be
interesting in how we end up

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:40.600
managing infrastructure,
is it doesn't really

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.180
need DNS load balancing.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:44.350
We don't need to put
a DNS load balancer

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.440
in front of the load balancer.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:49.720
The load balancer itself takes
care of both geolocation,

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:52.500
as well as load balancing
across your multiple instances.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:54.940
And you don't need to
worry about that at all.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:58.210
And API-wise, the
way this looks is you

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:02.400
set up a global forwarding
rule, which is basically an IP.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.590
And that forwards to
different forms of proxies,

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:10.990
whether it's a TCP proxy, or
HTTP proxy, or HTTPS proxy.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:14.650
Which then, in turn,
forwards to different forms

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:16.780
of instance groups.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.670
Now, here's where it
becomes interesting.

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:22.390
The way we recommend running
instance groups is basically

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.960
have instance groups that span--

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:28.510
that are actually
in different zones.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:30.280
And this actually
comes from a history

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:32.200
of running infrastructure.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:34.900
Fundamentally, when you want
to build a highly reliable

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:40.210
application, really running
it across multiple regions

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:42.790
is the best way of
getting reliability.

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:44.650
It's much better
than if you run it

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:47.410
across multiple zones in the
region, which is definitely

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:49.870
much more recommended
than just relying

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:51.300
on a single zone in the region.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:52.150
Right?

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:54.710
As an example of this,
when Hurricane Matthew

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:58.410
hit the East Coast last--

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:01.900
when Hurricane Matthew hit
the East Coast last year,

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:05.590
it actually passed over
our US East data center.

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:10.030
And none of you actually--
if you were in that region,

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:11.410
you did not get an outage.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:14.050
Yes, we had taken a
lot of precautions

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:17.680
about actually making
sure that the building is

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:20.050
resilient to a hurricane,
because it's a hurricane zone.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:21.758
But ensured that there
was adequate fuel.

00:11:21.758 --> 00:11:26.500
We ensured that low priority
traffic was actually routed off

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.452
the network so
that if we actually

00:11:28.452 --> 00:11:30.160
had some network
failures, it would still

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:31.330
be able to carry traffic.

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:33.820
But even then, in
spite of all of that,

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:36.940
if ever you have something
which takes out a region,

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:40.900
you expect to lose about eight
hours or so, which would mean,

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:43.131
essentially, you would lose
your phone lines in like

00:11:43.131 --> 00:11:44.380
a [? 10-year ?] period, right?

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:47.800
So spreading your instances
across multiple zones

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.660
is definitely something
that we recommend,

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:53.070
and something that really we
do across the board in Google.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.720
And once you have a
load balancer set up,

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:03.970
you can actually use
that for failover.

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:07.000
The load balancer itself
is quite tolerant of--

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.390
it has some failure
mechanisms that are embedded.

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:14.740
As an example of this, when it's
pointing to different instance

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:18.580
groups in different zones, if
one of those instance groups

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:21.980
decides to be unhealthy,
for whatever reason,

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:24.780
then the load
balancer automatically

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:27.190
marks that unhealthy, stops
routing traffic to that,

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:30.250
and routes traffic
to everything else.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:31.914
Now, we can use this--

00:12:31.914 --> 00:12:33.580
again, while this is
automatic behavior,

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:38.470
this is a very useful
technique to even route traffic

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:39.940
away when you have a problem.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:42.010
Like when you notice a
problem before the load

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:43.330
balancer notices a problem.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:46.750
And we use this quite
frequently to drain traffic away

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:47.810
across multiple regions.

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:50.920
So as an example, say you
have an app that's replicated

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.070
across multiple zones.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.110
And in general,
say you have set it

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:58.820
up such that you have
n plus 1, because you

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:02.860
are able to take any particular
instance group down, but still

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:04.870
be able to actually
use your capacity

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:06.697
and not lose availability.

00:13:06.697 --> 00:13:08.530
One of the things you
can do if you actually

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:10.240
are doing a rollout
and you notice

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:14.870
a problem with a particular
zone, drain traffic away.

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:17.480
You can basically
use our API to set

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:19.040
the capacity of
that particular zone

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:22.190
to zero, which immediately
starts routing--

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:24.770
asks the load balancer to route
traffic to the other zones.

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:27.370
Now, existing connections
will remain in flight.

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:30.650
Of course, if your backends
are down, those will be reset.

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:32.720
But any new connections
and any new queries

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:35.900
will just go to the new zones.

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:38.120
Notice that this is
actually much better

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:41.570
than if you were trying
to do this with a DNS load

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:42.530
balancing in front.

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:44.990
Because if you were trying
to do this with DNS,

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:46.820
you would need to--

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:49.220
you'd be subject to DNS TTLs.

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:52.100
So say you had a
TTL of five minutes,

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.840
for about five
minutes, everybody

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:55.730
who was trying to--
till the TTL expires,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:58.620
and your zone records
change, everybody

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:02.790
who's coming to your website's
getting, essentially,

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:08.510
has a chance of going to the
bad zone and getting issues.

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:11.390
This philosophy actually
also lines up very well

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:12.980
with a higher level
mantra that we

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:18.470
have in SRE, which is
around essentially having

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:23.390
an easy button whenever
you actually face problems.

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:27.340
If you ever need to debug an
issue in response to an outage,

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:28.780
you're wasting about 30 minutes.

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:29.280
Really.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:32.240
I mean, most people, we've
either designed the system

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:33.830
to automatically
deal with problems,

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:37.250
or if it is clearly human
and the human is actually

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:40.190
debugging issues, you've
lost 30 minutes of downtime.

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:41.915
So typically, what
we do internally

00:14:41.915 --> 00:14:43.790
whenever we face any
issue is the first thing

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:45.170
you do is head drain.

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:46.920
Right Normally you
have a zonal outage.

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:49.590
You immediately-- the
people actually just head

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:52.940
drain and continue.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:57.920
And we use this very regularly
doing the rollouts as well,

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:03.710
as an example of one operation
wherein we use drains.

00:15:03.710 --> 00:15:06.380
So one of the things
we noticed when

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:10.130
we were doing a deep analysis
of where we were getting outages

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:13.910
from is we were getting
most of our outages

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:16.410
as a result of rollouts
and config changes.

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:19.290
About 70% of them were
coming as a result of that.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:23.150
And when we see
something like that,

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:24.800
the answer can't
be stop doing it,

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:27.440
because if you're
in IT, if you're

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:29.990
running an organization
which is thriving on change,

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:32.090
the last thing you want
to do for your business

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:33.690
is stop making changes.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:36.322
So making changes is really an
important part of the thing.

00:15:36.322 --> 00:15:38.030
But at the same time,
being able to do it

00:15:38.030 --> 00:15:42.590
in a fashion which doesn't cause
outages is equally important.

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:46.070
And the ability to
actually move traffic away,

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:48.070
to drain traffic away
whenever you see a problem

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:53.240
is a key part of improving
your recovery time.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:54.890
So as an example of
one of the things

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:58.730
we do when we actually do
a rollout is we typically

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:01.160
update or push conflicts
to a single zone.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:04.100
We treat our zone as a
unit of failure internally.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.772
And we do A/B tests there.

00:16:06.772 --> 00:16:08.480
You can use a similar
philosophy even you

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.790
are doing a green/blue rollout.

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:14.870
Or if you're upgrading
parts, subparts of your zone,

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:16.010
we do an A/B test.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:20.350
If you actually see--
anytime when you have, say,

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:23.060
your CPU load goes up,
or your latency goes up,

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:24.590
just drain out of that cell.

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:26.830
And then you can come back,
figure out what's wrong,

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:30.080
do a rollback, analyze,
move forward again.

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:32.405
This really helps
improve the MTTR.

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:38.960
As an example of this, once
what happened is there was--

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:41.290
this is before we
actually audited,

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:44.600
went through a lot of things
to eliminate global config

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:45.470
pushes--

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:47.780
there was once a config
push which happened,

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:49.910
which basically was starting
to get a large number

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:51.470
of our front ends down.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:53.930
It paged a person,
luckily on the same floor.

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:55.940
And the person who made
that push immediately,

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:58.910
just because he heard
a noise, drained back.

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:03.320
And that actually-- in analysis,
if that person had not done it,

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:07.475
and we would have tried to
debug it after the fact,

00:17:07.475 --> 00:17:10.099
we estimated that we would have
taken about 45 minutes to debug

00:17:10.099 --> 00:17:11.660
this problem.

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:17.420
So in that example of having
the easy button whenever you're

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:19.690
doing changes is very powerful.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:22.490
In both actually
allowing for velocity,

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:27.130
at the same time
improving reliability.

00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:28.801
But a lot of this
doesn't actually--

00:17:28.801 --> 00:17:30.550
I mean, there are
definitely corner cases.

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:32.890
I don't want to paint a
picture where you just

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:35.020
use load balancing and
everything magically just

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:36.484
works and always keeps working.

00:17:36.484 --> 00:17:37.900
There are interesting
corner cases

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:42.914
that you need to worry
about whenever you're

00:17:42.914 --> 00:17:44.830
running a large scale
distribution system that

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:47.050
spans multiple zones.

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:51.580
And let's talk of two
interesting cases.

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:54.640
The first one is, basically,
cascading failures.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:58.810
This is something that brought
Gmail down for a little while

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:02.470
very early in Gmail's history.

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:03.590
The case is very simple.

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:07.480
Again, you just haven't capacity
planned your capacity plan

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.636
provision very well.

00:18:09.636 --> 00:18:11.260
You've decided to
have your application

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:14.230
across multiple
zones, but you're just

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:17.990
pushed to a single zone,
and that zone goes down.

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:20.330
And immediately, a load
balancer realizes, aha,

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:21.670
that zone is down.

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:24.370
Let me route all the
traffic to the other zones.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:26.440
But you have not capacity
planned properly,

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:30.460
and this is happening
during a time

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:31.930
when there's too much traffic.

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.472
And that, in turn,
brings another zone down.

00:18:34.472 --> 00:18:35.680
Now you see a problem, right?

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:39.420
You end up bringing
your entire site down.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:42.230
The interesting thing
about situations like this

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:45.530
is once you reach a
situation like this,

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:47.810
it's actually
difficult to recover

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:52.730
when you have users beating down
on your doors to get in, right?

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:58.730
This is actually-- casually,
occasionally try to say this.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:01.340
This is trying to restart
an engine on an airplane

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:03.050
when it's actually crashing.

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:05.820
It's really hard when you're
being buffeted by all the wind.

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:08.720
And this is basically trying
to do a cold start when

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:10.960
you have hordes at your door.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:12.790
And one of the things
we realize when you--

00:19:12.790 --> 00:19:15.290
to do, basically,
what happens is

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:16.670
you try to bring
one instance up.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:17.870
That comes up.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:20.670
Again, your load balancer
says, aha, some instance is up,

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:21.830
everybody go there.

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:22.460
Guess what.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:24.050
That goes down instantaneously.

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:28.460
You bring another instance up,
that goes on instantaneously.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:30.270
We've actually,
again, over time, we

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:33.400
have worked out features
and we've worked out ideas

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:35.690
to deal with
situations like this.

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:38.720
Examples of this include
load testing standard.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:40.550
Like when you
capacity provision,

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:42.470
make sure you have n plus 1.

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:43.700
That's the easy one.

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:45.500
Other interesting
cases are things

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:49.500
like have degraded failure
modes, wherein, essentially--

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:51.560
and this we use
across of search pages

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:53.060
and across of static pages.

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:55.160
So when you get too
much load, don't

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:57.935
try to just get rid of the
more complex dynamic pages.

00:19:57.935 --> 00:19:59.220
Just server your static page.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:01.250
This is something
that CNN learned.

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:04.130
I believe, in one of
their postmortems,

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:07.280
they wrote about
this after the 9/11--

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:09.097
when 9/11 happened,
they actually

00:20:09.097 --> 00:20:11.180
had a traffic surge which
brought their site down.

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:13.180
And they moved to actually
serving static pages.

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:17.350
Again, an example of a
graceful degradation.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:22.100
Another thing that
you can do is, again,

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:25.790
using the load balancer, you
can try to black hole traffic

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:29.090
at a single place so that
you can restart your engine,

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:31.210
start sending a little
bit of traffic in there.

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:32.660
[INAUDIBLE] a
little bit more air.

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:34.460
Once that boots up
completely, putting up

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:35.870
the next instance, and so on.

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:39.020
Once you're able to handle
that, bring up the entire site.

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:41.165
So these are all techniques
that we have learned.

00:20:41.165 --> 00:20:42.980
We have talked
about in the future,

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:48.170
we incorporate that in
our customer engagements.

00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:51.720
And we've written about
this in our books as well.

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:55.260
So just to summarize
the key messages,

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:58.970
the load balancer is actually
a powerful reliability tool.

00:20:58.970 --> 00:20:59.930
You should use it.

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:03.220
I mean, it's actually very
useful across a variety

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:06.200
of operations, in addition
to load balancing.

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:07.770
It's very powerful.

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:10.277
There are definitely
interesting corner cases.

00:21:10.277 --> 00:21:12.110
They're definitely
worthwhile talking about,

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:15.260
because again, most people,
when they run websites,

00:21:15.260 --> 00:21:18.230
it's very rare that you
have a very stable traffic

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:22.430
pattern that doesn't change day
in day out, across the year.

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:24.980
You need to really be aware
of what happens at peak.

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:27.540
What happens when an
instance goes down.

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:29.810
These are kind of things
I think we actually

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:34.912
use in our engagements with
our customers, right, Luke?

00:21:34.912 --> 00:21:37.380
LUKE STONE: Yes, exactly.

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:42.990
So the trick is to take
these gems of wisdom

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:46.290
that SREs have
developed over the years

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:50.310
with running Google systems
and try to actually apply them

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:52.564
to our customer systems.

00:21:52.564 --> 00:21:54.480
Teach people how to use
the technology that we

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:56.960
have to get the most out of it.

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:59.370
Because you don't just
kind of automatically

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:01.860
get resilience from plugging
yourself into this load

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:02.580
balancer product.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:04.110
You actually have to
know how to operate it,

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:06.420
and you have to be able
to know how your system is

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:08.550
going to respond behind it.

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:12.510
So what we did with
Home Depot is we

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:15.970
got non-abstract with them.

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:16.560
Right?

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:19.230
So we have a concept
in SRE hiring

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:21.480
which is called
non-abstract system design.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:25.440
And if anybody here has gone
through an SRE interview,

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:26.690
you know about this.

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:28.620
Basically we say,
hey, would you like

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:32.550
to design a system for me
that's like a distributed hash

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:33.660
table or something?

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:37.590
And 90% of candidates say, oh
yeah, we'll have a hash table

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:39.020
and it'll work, whatever.

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:40.478
And then you kind
of say, OK, well,

00:22:40.478 --> 00:22:43.510
what if it has to
serve a million QPS?

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:47.340
And now people start thinking,
OK, well, what's each query?

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:48.799
What size is it going to be?

00:22:48.799 --> 00:22:50.340
How many backends
am I going to have?

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:51.756
What bandwidth am
I going to have?

00:22:51.756 --> 00:22:54.696
And you say, OK, what if it
has to serve a billion QPS?

00:22:54.696 --> 00:22:56.070
And you watch how
people actually

00:22:56.070 --> 00:23:00.510
apply these numbers to
their specific design

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:04.400
in order to detect failure
modes and then make

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:07.650
a modification to the
design to make it more

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:09.960
resilient to this failure mode.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:11.989
So we do that all the
time in interviewing.

00:23:11.989 --> 00:23:13.530
And the reason is
because we actually

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:16.530
have to do it all the
time in real life as well.

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:21.510
And this is what CRE
is doing by bringing

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:24.900
the wisdom and practices
of SRE into our customer's

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:25.410
environment.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:28.860
We take these lessons
and we just apply them.

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:33.740
So it kind of goes in
three-step process.

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:35.490
First thing we have
to do is we have to go

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:37.350
and listen and learn
and figure out all

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:39.970
about their application.

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:44.970
Otherwise, you don't really know
what your non-abstract system

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:46.651
is that you're working with.

00:23:46.651 --> 00:23:48.150
The next thing you
have to do is you

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:51.120
have to analyze each
component of the system using

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.580
numbers and figuring out
what are the tradeoffs

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:55.110
and where it's going to break.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:58.100
And the final thing you
do is design mitigations

00:23:58.100 --> 00:23:59.850
and execute them, so
that you can actually

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:02.710
make a resilient system.

00:24:02.710 --> 00:24:04.290
So that's what we
did at Home Depot.

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:08.130
And I'll tell you a little
bit about how it went.

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:12.200
First things first is
we just went to Atlanta.

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:17.010
This is a picture of AJ, who
is one of our CRE engineers.

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:20.502
And we're in Home Depot's
Store Support Center.

00:24:20.502 --> 00:24:21.210
Isn't that great?

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:23.876
That gives you a little sense of
the Home Depot company culture.

00:24:23.876 --> 00:24:26.310
They call their corporate
headquarters the Store Support

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:27.720
Center.

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:29.420
And you can see-- we
look out the window

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:31.710
and you can see a
Home Depot store 121

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:32.970
right across the street.

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:34.170
So we went to the store.

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:36.030
We checked out how
things were going.

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:38.880
We walk the floor
with the engineers.

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:42.670
And we kind of see what's
their culture a little bit,

00:24:42.670 --> 00:24:45.030
the culture of this SRE team
that William's building.

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:45.480
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah.

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:47.580
And you guys spending
the time with us there,

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:50.280
I think we spent probably
the better part of--

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:53.000
I mean, you were there on
site a couple of times,

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:55.560
but spent the better
part of three months

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.000
kind of working
actively with you guys.

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:01.020
And your engagement-- one thing
I actually wanted to mention,

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:05.735
you were talking about this
non-abstract system design.

00:25:05.735 --> 00:25:07.110
I've done a number
of engagements

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:09.630
over the years with
other companies

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:12.630
have come in and
done some consulting.

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:15.030
And generally, the type of
consulting advice you'll get

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:17.250
will be fairly generic
architectural kind

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:19.320
of recommendations
that could be useful.

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:21.240
Tend to be more
application oriented,

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:24.030
or could be this library
or that, and so forth.

00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:25.290
This monitoring and that.

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.510
But what you guys did,
bringing that practices

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:29.710
that Mahesh is talking
about, is the real deal.

00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:32.197
I mean, you guys are dealing
with much larger systems

00:25:32.197 --> 00:25:33.780
than we're dealing
with at Home Depot.

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:35.370
But we're still big enough.

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:39.240
And you guys are bringing pretty
impressive deep understanding

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:43.410
of resiliency and failure
points and so forth that really

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:46.590
rubbed off on Home
Depot's SRE team.

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:48.390
We actually looked at
you guys as mentors,

00:25:48.390 --> 00:25:51.090
kind of helped us understand
the things we should be doing.

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:53.600
For example, a
wheel of misfortune.

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:57.000
And I interact with
a lot of other people

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.130
in other similar
companies that aren't

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:03.480
really tech first companies,
like us, we're a retail first.

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:06.210
And doing something like a
regular wheel of misfortune

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:07.934
exercise, we do it weekly.

00:26:07.934 --> 00:26:09.600
This is something we
learned from Google

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:11.060
where we set up a scenario.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:13.090
It could be in our
stage environment,

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:15.900
and sometimes it's in our
production environment.

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:19.350
And we do the same sort of thing
where we set up a scenario,

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:23.460
put an SRE, or even a developer
in front of a command line

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:25.920
and see how they perform.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:28.036
When we're done, we do
a blameless post-mortem

00:26:28.036 --> 00:26:29.160
and we learn a lot from it.

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:30.330
But that takes structure.

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:32.710
That takes planning to do that.

00:26:32.710 --> 00:26:33.910
It doesn't happen naturally.

00:26:33.910 --> 00:26:38.070
So you don't see that very
often in most enterprise

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:40.620
environments, but it
really pays off for us.

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:44.412
So I think just you guys being
around was good mentorship.

00:26:44.412 --> 00:26:45.120
LUKE STONE: Yeah.

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:47.635
I remember one story of
a wheel of misfortune

00:26:47.635 --> 00:26:50.010
we were doing where somebody
was sitting back and saying,

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:51.270
oh, that's so interesting.

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:53.360
The traffic's not
coming into zone A,

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:56.520
but it is coming into zone
B. And it's very interesting

00:26:56.520 --> 00:26:58.440
that we're losing
50% of our traffic.

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:00.900
WILLIAM BONNELL: Don't
admire the problem.

00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:01.260
LUKE STONE: That's
what some said.

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:02.570
Don't admire the problem.

00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:03.600
Hit rollback.

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:05.660
And then I was like,
OK, they're getting it.

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:07.650
Now they're getting it.

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:08.669
So that was cool.

00:27:08.669 --> 00:27:10.710
So we just went to Atlanta
for just a couple days

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:12.376
just to kind of get
the lay of the land.

00:27:12.376 --> 00:27:15.450
And then we get into the
meat of our CRE engagement,

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:18.660
which is to do this thing we
call an application reliability

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:19.530
review.

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:23.970
And it's based off of Google's
internal production readiness

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:26.040
reviews, which is what
we put every Google

00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:29.910
product through before it
launches to the public.

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:32.430
And we have some
specialist SREs who

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:34.530
are really good at looking
through your design

00:27:34.530 --> 00:27:36.570
docs and all your
readiness procedures

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:40.140
and identifying the
gaps before you go live.

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:44.430
And we have some of those
veterans from that team

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:47.790
on our CRE team now.

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:48.755
So we ran this thing.

00:27:48.755 --> 00:27:50.130
And what you do
is you go through

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:52.560
and you identify what
are some of the most

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:56.310
important components, like
monitoring is always on there.

00:27:56.310 --> 00:27:57.840
Deployments is always on there.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:27:59.520
Like Mahesh said,
most of your problems

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:02.400
come when you actually
change something.

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:04.920
And we did load balancing,
because Home Depot,

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:09.330
very impressively, had set up
multi-zonal and multi-regional

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:11.250
configuration with
their backends.

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:16.320
So this procedure was led by
our tech lead, Matt Brown, who

00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:19.470
happens to be an expert
in load balancing

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:21.870
and worked on Google
SRE's traffic team

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:23.160
for a number of years.

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:25.920
And basically, met with
the technical experts

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:28.530
at Home Depot on all of
these systems for a couple

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:31.110
hours a day for several weeks.

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:34.310
And really pick apart how
does this work, what's

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:35.490
your procedure going to be?

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:39.000
And we were able to
identify some failure

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:40.920
modes that weren't covered.

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:44.250
So for example, if
you lose a region,

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:46.380
then you're going
to instantaneously

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:49.500
get all that region's traffic,
boom, onto your remaining

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:50.520
region, right?

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:54.600
But the autoscaler was set
to scale at 60% of CPU.

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:57.965
So if you take that traffic
and thump it over to this guy,

00:28:57.965 --> 00:28:59.340
you're going to
saturate the CPU.

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:00.756
So we're like,
obviously, you need

00:29:00.756 --> 00:29:02.970
to have the autoscaler
set at less than 50%.

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:08.160
That's just like one
really obvious example.

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:13.140
But we came up with a
list of about 20 things

00:29:13.140 --> 00:29:14.700
and we color coded them.

00:29:14.700 --> 00:29:16.050
These are risks we identified.

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:18.930
Color coded them red,
orange, and green

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:20.650
in terms of their severity.

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:23.460
And then we had a big meeting
where we got everybody together

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:26.770
across all the systems,
both Home Depot and Google.

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:28.810
And we kind of said,
OK, here's where we are.

00:29:28.810 --> 00:29:31.460
Here's all the gaps
that we've identified

00:29:31.460 --> 00:29:33.120
that are major
risks that we have

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:35.460
to face before Black Friday.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:38.522
And we got about six weeks,
at this point, to do it?

00:29:38.522 --> 00:29:40.230
WILLIAM BONNELL: And
actually, I remember

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:45.600
when Dave Resin and you
first met with us pitching

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.850
the idea of customer
reliability and engineering,

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:49.750
one of the things you
said to us was, hey,

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:52.650
we're going to deep dive
into your infrastructure

00:29:52.650 --> 00:29:53.609
and find some problems.

00:29:53.609 --> 00:29:55.525
And we're going to
classify them, as you said,

00:29:55.525 --> 00:29:56.871
high, medium, or low.

00:29:56.871 --> 00:29:59.370
But in order for us to share a
fate with you-- and by shared

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:01.800
fate, what Luke and Dave were
saying at the time is, hey,

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:03.216
we'll be in the
war room with you.

00:30:03.216 --> 00:30:05.340
We'll be in your Black
Friday war room with you.

00:30:05.340 --> 00:30:07.740
But you've got to pass
our reliability review.

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:11.340
So any high risk items we
find, you've got to remediate.

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:13.500
So we kind of went
into it knowing

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:15.150
that that was our agreement.

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:16.795
What I was impressed
with was not

00:30:16.795 --> 00:30:18.420
only did we have
action items, you guys

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:20.452
actually had action items too.

00:30:20.452 --> 00:30:21.160
LUKE STONE: Yeah.

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:25.930
Like for example,
we wanted to set up

00:30:25.930 --> 00:30:29.650
monitoring across the
system so that both SRE

00:30:29.650 --> 00:30:31.840
teams, both our SRE team
and William's SRE team,

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:35.120
could see the same metrics
on the same pane of glass.

00:30:35.120 --> 00:30:37.810
This cuts down on the
amount of confusion.

00:30:37.810 --> 00:30:40.930
And talking back and forth
over support tickets,

00:30:40.930 --> 00:30:43.660
it just totally
takes minutes away

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:48.080
from any kind of response rate.

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:50.650
And we took back some other
feedback to the product teams

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:52.270
on the load balancer.

00:30:52.270 --> 00:30:54.040
Like for example,
they were intending

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:57.880
to failover to a different
zone by adding another instance

00:30:57.880 --> 00:30:59.780
group to the load balancer.

00:30:59.780 --> 00:31:02.140
But the load bouncer
takes a little while

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:04.000
to start health
checking and actually

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:05.291
setting up the instance script.

00:31:05.291 --> 00:31:07.510
And so even if that's
your plan, then your plan

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:09.730
includes five
minutes of downtime,

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:12.870
or something like that.

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:15.700
So those are the kind of
things we took back and forth

00:31:15.700 --> 00:31:16.767
and worked on.

00:31:16.767 --> 00:31:18.850
WILLIAM BONNELL: And I'll
mention one other thing.

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:20.900
We talk a lot about how
hot/hot and multi-region,

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:21.820
that was actually something--

00:31:21.820 --> 00:31:23.260
I was talking about
the monolith earlier

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:24.260
and I forgot to mention.

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:24.926
It's important.

00:31:24.926 --> 00:31:27.050
One of the things, we took
this golden opportunity.

00:31:27.050 --> 00:31:28.900
I mentioned we
chipped away and built

00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:30.400
a microservice architecture.

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:31.690
We built everything hot/hot.

00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:34.630
So any customer facing
transactions we have,

00:31:34.630 --> 00:31:36.250
we actually build
it to run across

00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:38.680
not just multi-zones
in a region,

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:40.806
but actually across
multi-regions.

00:31:40.806 --> 00:31:43.180
So I think we did a lot better
on your reliability review

00:31:43.180 --> 00:31:45.070
than we probably
would have otherwise.

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:45.780
LUKE STONE: Yeah.

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:49.090
You kind of aced it.

00:31:49.090 --> 00:31:54.070
So we design SRE within Google
to be a very scarce resource.

00:31:54.070 --> 00:32:02.250
And that makes the product teams
value it and actually respect

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:04.920
the recommendations
that SREs are giving.

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:06.420
Does that makes sense?

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:09.011
So we kind of said
if you want us

00:32:09.011 --> 00:32:11.010
to go in the war room
with you for Black Friday,

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:16.140
then you need to implement these
recommendations as a condition.

00:32:16.140 --> 00:32:18.270
And you guys executed on it.

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:21.630
And we worked around
your production freeze.

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:24.730
And you got all the stuff
done to the point where

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:26.580
we said, OK, yes, let's do it.

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:29.785
So lets talk about how we
prepared for the actual event.

00:32:29.785 --> 00:32:32.160
WILLIAM BONNELL: And something
like Black Friday, anybody

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:34.680
that works in retail
can appreciate

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:38.441
how important an event like
Black Friday is, or could be.

00:32:38.441 --> 00:32:40.440
Maybe you're a marketing
company, an advertising

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:40.940
company.

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:42.630
You might have the Super Bowl.

00:32:42.630 --> 00:32:45.990
You could be in education and so
forth and have your peak days.

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:48.750
So for us in retail it's
Black Friday, Cyber Monday,

00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:49.560
even Thanksgiving.

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:51.450
We pulled a lot of
it into Thanksgiving.

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:52.477
Something we're good at.

00:32:52.477 --> 00:32:53.310
It's tried and true.

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:55.530
We do it-- been
doing it for years.

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:58.920
I've personally been doing it
at Home Depot for 11 years.

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:00.480
We start about six
months in advance.

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:02.760
We do regular
production load testing,

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:04.980
production destructive testing.

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:07.920
We do a code freeze couple of
weeks leading up to the event

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:09.420
just so nothing goes wrong.

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.220
War room, 24/7.

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:13.470
It's a really pretty big deal.

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:16.410
But this year,
this past year, we

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:20.070
had our tried and true legacy
platform, but we've also

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:22.320
got very important
critical services

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:24.100
that are running in
Google at that point.

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:27.450
So it was a pretty
complex year, thinking

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:30.880
about running both our on prem
platform as well as in Google.

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:32.407
I was pretty nervous.

00:33:32.407 --> 00:33:33.990
LUKE STONE: I was
kind of nervous too,

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:36.405
because we hadn't
done this either.

00:33:39.120 --> 00:33:43.440
But I don't know, we had worked
together on this as a team.

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:47.400
And I felt responsible
for the application too.

00:33:47.400 --> 00:33:51.220
And we had followed all
of our SRE best practices,

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:52.060
as well as we could.

00:33:52.060 --> 00:33:54.420
So we didn't really
have any reason

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:57.750
to doubt that we were
going to perform well.

00:33:57.750 --> 00:34:01.170
So we started checking
the boxes in terms

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:06.360
of getting in a war room has
AV connectivity and real time

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:10.139
communications, setting
up all the monitoring so

00:34:10.139 --> 00:34:12.239
that everybody can see
all the monitoring,

00:34:12.239 --> 00:34:16.080
and working through playbooks
and scenarios about,

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:18.449
what should we do if
this happens, right?

00:34:18.449 --> 00:34:21.600
So kind of worked
through all the stuff.

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:24.570
And I felt confident
to put my pager

00:34:24.570 --> 00:34:28.139
on the primary on-call for it.

00:34:28.139 --> 00:34:32.760
And then we sat down
on Thursday night

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:38.730
to kind of watch the graphs,
and watch the orders roll in.

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:44.340
And it was pretty
exciting, because I really

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:50.550
could feel that it's important
to have trust in your cloud

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:51.570
provider.

00:34:51.570 --> 00:34:54.510
I could tell that you guys
were giving up a lot of control

00:34:54.510 --> 00:34:58.530
from going off of your monolith
where you could-- there's

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:01.740
someone to call and yell at
and fire if they screw it up,

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:02.700
right?

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:05.360
And it's a different
deal in public cloud.

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:08.370
So I was really working hard
to win your trust so that you

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:12.540
could go through with
this proof of concept

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:14.620
and this test on Black Friday.

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:16.280
So what happened?

00:35:18.794 --> 00:35:19.835
WILLIAM BONNELL: Boredom.

00:35:22.710 --> 00:35:26.730
We have a saying in our
Home Depot SRE team.

00:35:26.730 --> 00:35:31.140
If at any moment during the five
day event, or the other events

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:34.560
we have like that, you would
ask yourself, why am I here,

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:37.890
do I really need to be here,
we've achieved success.

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:39.960
We've done what
we set out to do.

00:35:39.960 --> 00:35:40.890
So it's boredom.

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:43.890
Actually, I put a little
blog post up here.

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:45.700
When we hire somebody
new into the team,

00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:48.570
we always indoctrinate them
very early on about Black Friday

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:49.812
and how important it is.

00:35:49.812 --> 00:35:52.020
And of course, we don't take
vacation on Black Friday

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:53.103
because that's a peak day.

00:35:53.103 --> 00:35:55.870
Even on Thanksgiving, many of
us are working on Thanksgiving.

00:35:55.870 --> 00:36:00.120
So really kind of have scary
horror stories about it.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:03.390
And we hired a
SRE out of college

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:05.280
who had participated
in this event.

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:08.260
And I just put his blog post
here up on the screen here.

00:36:08.260 --> 00:36:10.290
It just said, all the
horror stories you heard,

00:36:10.290 --> 00:36:12.530
all the wheel of misfortune,
all the prep we did,

00:36:12.530 --> 00:36:13.462
what was the result?

00:36:13.462 --> 00:36:15.420
Really, just kind of sat
around in the war room

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:16.710
and watched the money come in.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:18.540
So that was success for us.

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:23.460
LUKE STONE: Yeah, I was really
impressed with something

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:26.790
that Naveen said afterwards,
which was that not only did you

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:32.280
have record traffic,
record orders and revenue,

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:37.320
and record up time, but you
also had record amount of change

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:38.564
going into the system.

00:36:38.564 --> 00:36:40.230
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah,
not only just all

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:42.188
the changes, all the
microservices and whatnot,

00:36:42.188 --> 00:36:44.740
but as I mentioned, running both
on our on prem and in cloud,

00:36:44.740 --> 00:36:47.054
it was a very complex
architecture this year.

00:36:47.054 --> 00:36:48.720
I think the scariest
thing that happened

00:36:48.720 --> 00:36:53.690
to us that whole event was false
Stackdriver alerts on Monday.

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:55.500
That was kind of scary
for a little while,

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:57.060
but that was the worst
thing that happened.

00:36:57.060 --> 00:36:59.184
LUKE STONE: Yeah, all the
pagers started going off.

00:36:59.184 --> 00:37:02.280
But then we had the monitoring
set up, so we were able to--

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:04.952
we got on Slack, and we
got on the monitoring,

00:37:04.952 --> 00:37:06.660
and we took a look at
it, and said, look,

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:08.250
this doesn't look like
it's a real problem.

00:37:08.250 --> 00:37:09.416
It's probably a false alert.

00:37:09.416 --> 00:37:13.730
And then I was able to get in
touch with the Stackdriver SRE

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:17.850
team, and they said, yeah,
this is a condition that

00:37:17.850 --> 00:37:19.489
would create a false
alert of the kind

00:37:19.489 --> 00:37:20.530
that you're experiencing.

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:21.863
So I was able to pass that back.

00:37:21.863 --> 00:37:24.092
Yeah, it's a known
issue and it's just

00:37:24.092 --> 00:37:25.050
affecting the alerting.

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:28.229
It's not affecting
traffic or anything.

00:37:28.229 --> 00:37:29.520
So that worked out pretty well.

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:32.090
There was another
issue too where

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:36.390
there was a third party
service that was outside

00:37:36.390 --> 00:37:38.560
of both of our systems.

00:37:38.560 --> 00:37:42.000
It was just a third party
API that you were calling to.

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:44.910
And then it was some
kind of e-commerce API.

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:47.400
And it predictively
got [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:47.400 --> 00:37:48.700
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah, that
was in our recommendations.

00:37:48.700 --> 00:37:50.490
That was actually a good
example of degraded service

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:51.750
that Mahesh talked about.

00:37:51.750 --> 00:37:54.005
That's one of the
resilience concepts

00:37:54.005 --> 00:37:56.700
in your application, where
we depended on a third party

00:37:56.700 --> 00:37:57.430
service.

00:37:57.430 --> 00:38:00.480
That third party's service
have a little bit of a blip.

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:01.600
No big deal.

00:38:01.600 --> 00:38:04.419
We could just still
display the relevant pages.

00:38:04.419 --> 00:38:06.460
Just didn't have all the
information on the page.

00:38:06.460 --> 00:38:07.720
So it was no big deal.

00:38:07.720 --> 00:38:08.428
LUKE STONE: Yeah.

00:38:08.428 --> 00:38:14.370
We had discussed ahead of
time what our SLOs were

00:38:14.370 --> 00:38:17.010
and what services
were critical services

00:38:17.010 --> 00:38:19.230
and what services were
kind of OK services.

00:38:19.230 --> 00:38:21.900
I mean, I don't know what to
call them-- less critical.

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:24.480
The one in question was
a recommendation service.

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:26.970
Like if you like this
product, you might also

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:28.600
like these other products.

00:38:28.600 --> 00:38:30.066
So that was not critical.

00:38:30.066 --> 00:38:31.440
The main things
that are critical

00:38:31.440 --> 00:38:33.860
are like search, be
able to find products,

00:38:33.860 --> 00:38:36.870
be able to put things in
your cart and check out.

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:39.930
So once we realized that it
wasn't one of those services,

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:41.980
we knew ahead of
time that we weren't

00:38:41.980 --> 00:38:45.000
going to panic if this thing
broke because we had already

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:47.850
talked about our SLOs and
agreed on what was critical.

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:51.840
We were able to just kind
of wait it out and decide

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:55.820
not to react very much to it.

00:38:55.820 --> 00:38:58.620
It was pretty cool.

00:38:58.620 --> 00:39:01.340
So I was wondering,
William, how you

00:39:01.340 --> 00:39:06.230
felt that this experience
impacted your project to grow

00:39:06.230 --> 00:39:09.215
an SRE team within Home Depot?

00:39:09.215 --> 00:39:11.090
WILLIAM BONNELL: Well,
the impact I mentioned

00:39:11.090 --> 00:39:13.640
earlier was kind of
a good mentorship,

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:15.560
a good role model
in terms of how

00:39:15.560 --> 00:39:18.470
to think about running large
scale systems reliably.

00:39:18.470 --> 00:39:20.450
So that's actually
really paid off there.

00:39:20.450 --> 00:39:22.490
And we've had a
tremendous amount

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:26.000
of success running our platform
in the Google Cloud Platform.

00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:30.500
And so what's next for us
is continuing the migration

00:39:30.500 --> 00:39:33.244
of our platform into Google.

00:39:33.244 --> 00:39:34.910
LUKE STONE: So what's
that going to look

00:39:34.910 --> 00:39:36.980
like, and how far are you?

00:39:36.980 --> 00:39:39.470
WILLIAM BONNELL: It's
going to be busy.

00:39:39.470 --> 00:39:42.470
We're pretty far
along, but we've

00:39:42.470 --> 00:39:46.070
got some important
things still left to do.

00:39:46.070 --> 00:39:48.680
But I'd say we're
easily more than half--

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:51.030
we're 3/4 of the way through.

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:51.980
LUKE STONE: Yeah.

00:39:51.980 --> 00:39:53.750
I like your strategy.

00:39:53.750 --> 00:39:56.060
I think it's an effective
strategy of carving off

00:39:56.060 --> 00:40:01.160
microservices kind of one at
a time as you can stand up

00:40:01.160 --> 00:40:03.770
a development team to run them.

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:07.280
And then you basically have
your monolith calling out

00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:09.500
to the microservices
in cloud when it's

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:12.140
appropriate, and vice versa.

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:16.040
So you basically can glue these
things together with APIs,

00:40:16.040 --> 00:40:19.880
and then we can monitor
at that API layer.

00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:22.370
And it's really clear
what's going on.

00:40:22.370 --> 00:40:25.280
It's a pretty robust strategy
to do it incrementally.

00:40:25.280 --> 00:40:26.780
WILLIAM BONNELL:
And as I mentioned,

00:40:26.780 --> 00:40:29.530
just a plug for another Home
Depot session tomorrow, Ravi

00:40:29.530 --> 00:40:32.310
Yeddula is going to talk through
that, kind of the architecture.

00:40:32.310 --> 00:40:35.769
So anybody interested in how
we did that and that journey,

00:40:35.769 --> 00:40:36.560
please attend that.

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:38.690
The other plug is with you guys.

00:40:38.690 --> 00:40:40.940
And I didn't mention it, but
at 5 o'clock, we're doing

00:40:40.940 --> 00:40:43.430
the happy hour with you guys
at the [? Fifth ?] [? Nine. ?]

00:40:43.430 --> 00:40:45.138
And one of the things
you'll hear from us

00:40:45.138 --> 00:40:46.372
is standardized monitoring.

00:40:46.372 --> 00:40:48.830
And so since we have all these
different development teams,

00:40:48.830 --> 00:40:50.420
and they're all doing
different things

00:40:50.420 --> 00:40:52.730
and we're kind of driving
that independence,

00:40:52.730 --> 00:40:54.560
we wanted to make sure
that we standardize

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:55.370
in the way we monitor.

00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:56.786
Otherwise, there's
no way we could

00:40:56.786 --> 00:40:59.810
run all of these
services in totality

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:01.060
without some kind of standard.

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:03.393
So we'll talk a little bit
about that at the [? Fifth ?]

00:41:03.393 --> 00:41:04.132
[? Nine. ?]

00:41:04.132 --> 00:41:04.840
LUKE STONE: Yeah.

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:07.990
That's one of the
things that our SRE team

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:12.940
is going to be developing
over the next several months

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:15.790
or years, because remember,
SREs are software developers

00:41:15.790 --> 00:41:18.310
who are charged with keeping
the systems reliable.

00:41:18.310 --> 00:41:21.280
So we're going to
develop some software.

00:41:21.280 --> 00:41:24.520
And one of the big gaps
that we see in cloud

00:41:24.520 --> 00:41:26.920
is just this ability
to have a monitoring

00:41:26.920 --> 00:41:29.840
system that both sides can see.

00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:34.550
And it's like we can
see the Stackdriver

00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:36.220
dashboard's that you
guys have created,

00:41:36.220 --> 00:41:37.970
but we can't make
any sense of them.

00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:43.160
And anybody who has run
a reasonably large system

00:41:43.160 --> 00:41:45.530
on cloud and played
with a reasonably

00:41:45.530 --> 00:41:48.440
easy to use monitoring
system knows

00:41:48.440 --> 00:41:52.490
that pretty quickly you get 70
dashboards, and half of them

00:41:52.490 --> 00:41:54.630
are--

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:57.110
they're kind of-- nobody cares.

00:41:57.110 --> 00:41:59.690
And you have to really
be a day-to-day expert

00:41:59.690 --> 00:42:02.480
to know which ones
are important.

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:09.200
So our concept there is to work
together with your SRE team.

00:42:09.200 --> 00:42:11.810
Figure out which
metrics are going

00:42:11.810 --> 00:42:15.470
to be our important metrics
that we're going to use for SLOs

00:42:15.470 --> 00:42:16.670
and alerting.

00:42:16.670 --> 00:42:19.490
And then we're going to
only alert on those metrics.

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:22.820
So we don't alert if CPU
goes too high, right?

00:42:22.820 --> 00:42:26.630
We alert if checkouts
are not happening.

00:42:26.630 --> 00:42:28.859
If people are
getting server errors

00:42:28.859 --> 00:42:30.650
when they're trying to
put items in a cart.

00:42:30.650 --> 00:42:34.460
That's a user facing error,
and that's the type of thing

00:42:34.460 --> 00:42:36.650
we like to monitor in SRE.

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:39.620
You monitor the user pain.

00:42:39.620 --> 00:42:43.070
You don't monitor
a cause like CPU,

00:42:43.070 --> 00:42:45.770
because if it's not causing user
pain, then who cares, right?

00:42:45.770 --> 00:42:48.290
So we agree on
just a few metrics,

00:42:48.290 --> 00:42:52.000
like I think we chose
maybe four or five things.

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:55.760
It was kind of like load
balancer, traffic, errors,

00:42:55.760 --> 00:43:01.050
latency, classic SRE book stuff.

00:43:01.050 --> 00:43:06.240
And then we monitored
at the storage layer.

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:11.190
How slow are reads,
basically, from the storage.

00:43:11.190 --> 00:43:12.930
And then using
that technique, we

00:43:12.930 --> 00:43:15.240
kind of put the whole
application in somewhat

00:43:15.240 --> 00:43:18.690
of a black box, where
you guys could reason

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:21.180
about it inside the box,
and we could reason about it

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:23.550
outside the box.

00:43:23.550 --> 00:43:26.070
And there was a
nice, tight contract

00:43:26.070 --> 00:43:27.810
that we had between
our two teams

00:43:27.810 --> 00:43:29.920
about when we would
alert each other.

00:43:29.920 --> 00:43:30.690
That was cool.

00:43:30.690 --> 00:43:34.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

